{0 Requests and responses}

Read an incoming {!Workero.Request} and return a {!Workero.Response} from a
handler. Match the method and path to choose a response:

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match (Request.method_ req, URL.path (Request.url req)) with
  | GET, "/hello" -> Response.text "hello\n"
  | POST, "/echo" -> Response.text (Request.text req)
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Not_Found "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{1 Read a request}

{!Workero.Request.method_} returns a {!Workero.Method.t}. Use
{!Workero.Request.url} for a parsed URL, or {!Workero.Request.path} for a raw,
percent-encoded path. See {{!page-url}URLs} for query values and decoded paths.

{!Workero.Request.text} reads the entire body as UTF-8 text, replacing invalid
UTF-8. {!Workero.Request.bytes} reads raw bytes into a string. These readers
consume the body. To read it in chunks, get its stream with
{!Workero.Request.body}. Getting the stream does not itself consume it;
reading the stream does. A request with no body returns [None]. See
{{!page-streams}Streams} and {{!page-forms}Forms} for other body readers.

A body can be consumed only once. Call {!Workero.Request.clone} before reading
if you need two copies. {!Workero.Request.cf} returns Cloudflare request
properties, such as country and colo; individual properties can be absent.

{1 Work with headers}

Header names are case-insensitive. {!Workero.Request.header} and
{!Workero.Response.header} read a joined value. For an ordinary repeated
header, [header_all] returns a list with one joined value, not one item per
original header. For [set-cookie], {!Workero.Request.header_all} and
{!Workero.Response.header_all} return one item per cookie. Do not split cookies
at commas because a cookie can contain a comma.

{1 Send a response}

{!Workero.Response.text} sends text, {!Workero.Response.html} sends HTML, and
{!Workero.Response.make} sends raw bytes. Pass [~status] or [~headers] to set
them. Use [Response.empty `No_Content] when the response has no body, or
{!Workero.Response.redirect} for a redirect. See {{!page-streams}Streams} for
streamed responses and {{!page-json}JSON} for JSON responses.

Named no-body statuses ([`No_Content], [`Reset_Content], [`Not_Modified]) do
not type-check with the body constructors. A numeric [`Status n] still
type-checks: a status outside 200 to 599, or a non-empty body with status 204,
205, or 304, raises [Invalid_argument] at runtime. An empty string body with
one of these statuses sends no body. A [`Status 101] raises [Invalid_argument]
too. {!Workero.Status} has [`Continue] and [`Switching_Protocols] only to read
codes; use {!Workero.Websocket} for 101. See {!Workero.Status} for status
names.

To forward an upstream response with one additional header, retain its
original headers:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let upstream = fetch "https://example.com/" in
  let headers = Response.headers upstream @ [ ("x-via", "workero") ] in
  Response.copy ~headers upstream

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

[Response.copy ~headers] replaces the entire header list. Without [~headers],
it keeps the existing headers. The original and copy share one body stream;
only one can consume it. Use {!Workero.Response.clone} before reading if you
need two copies. See {{!page-fetch}Fetch} for outgoing requests.

See {!Workero.Request}, {!Workero.Response}, {!Workero.Method},
{!Workero.Status}, and {!Workero.Cf} for the complete interfaces.
